{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786504423,"nonce":"5I+7W4MNNF3Osxn60TcCIuasag0=","target":"9b8d22be65330a53e66651c6cfaf88ce138ae5fad04dba909ac76fa57f4233b8","message":"**What is wrong.** Every per-alert bound failure and every terminal batch failure is durably recorded, and nothing in the product can read it back. `internal/ingestion/repository/rejections.go` declares exactly two methods, `Record` and `RecordBatch` — both writes. `internal/ingestion/repository/batches.go` declares `Insert`, `Get`, `MarkProcessed` and `ResolveOrg`, and `Get` requires the caller to already know both `batch_id` and `received_at`, which only the job handler does. Of the 85 `operationId`s in `api/openapi/openapi.yaml`, none reads a rejection or lists a batch. Nothing in `web/src` consumes either table. The operator whose alert never appeared has `psql` as the only diagnostic.\n\n**The demonstration.** The migration that created the table already built the index for the screen. `db/migrations/00006_ingestion.sql:92` creates `ingest_rejections_source_idx ON ingest_rejections (org_id, source_id, received_at DESC)` under its own comment at `00006_ingestion.sql:90-91`: *\"Serves: the per-source rejection feed, newest first. This screen is the whole point of the table -- oto never silently drops (SPEC §C.9.1).\"* The repository doc comment repeats it, `rejections.go:19`: *\"the per-source rejection feed built on `ingest_rejections_source_idx` is the whole point of the table.\"* Index, partition key and sort order are all in place for a keyset read; the feed itself was never written. The single `SELECT` against `ingest_rejections` anywhere in `internal/` is `internal/drill/repository/artifacts.go:103`, which reads `LIMIT 20` rows scoped to one synthetic delivery drill's own `batch_id` — it can report on a drill you started, and cannot answer \"why did my alert not appear\".\n\n**Why it matters.** Three separate documents promise a read path in words that only a read path can satisfy. SPEC AC-6, `docs/design/SPEC.md:3771`: *\"An alert with 100 labels, or a 5 KiB label value, is **rejected into `ingest_rejections` with a visible reason and a metric** — never silently dropped.\"* AC-38, `SPEC.md:3846-3847`: *\"A webhook containing an alert with 100 labels, an invalid label name, or a `startsAt` in 2087 is recorded per-alert in `ingest_rejections` with a reason.\"* `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:207-208`: *\"Every one of them is also recorded as a rejection, so nothing disappears without a trace.\"* And CONTEXT.md:325-326 makes it a requirement rather than a nicety: *\"**Delivery failure must be visible per alert.** oto's silence must never be indistinguishable from \"no alert\".\"* On the ingest path, oto's silence is currently exactly that. The only surfaces an operator has are the 202 body's rejected count (`internal/ingestion/service/accept.go:47`) and `oto_ingest_rejected_total{reason}` — a rate by reason, which says that something was refused and never which label set.\n\nThis is cheaper than it looks. The durable record, the redacted `raw` element, the closed reason enum and the covering index all exist; only the read is missing. **No migration is required** — `ingest_rejections_source_idx` already keys `(org_id, source_id, received_at DESC)`, which is the keyset the feed needs.\n\n**Where.** `internal/ingestion/repository/rejections.go` (write-only), `internal/ingestion/repository/batches.go` (no list, no failed-batch query), `db/migrations/00006_ingestion.sql:92` (the index that exists for this), `internal/drill/repository/artifacts.go:103` (the one existing read, drill-scoped), `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` (no operation), `web/src` (no consumer).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** `GET /api/v1/sources/{id}/rejections` returns a keyset page riding `ingest_rejections_source_idx`, filterable by `reason`, carrying the rejected label set from `raw` alongside `reason` and `detail`; a failed or `partial` `ingest_batch` is listable with its `error`; and a panel on the source screen shows both, so the answer to \"my alert never appeared\" is in the product rather than in `psql`.","files":null}]}